WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.280
Let’s say you’re trying to
find some solid information

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.780
about a serious health
problem that concerns you,

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.440
like high blood pressure,
or diabetes.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.080
Yet, everywhere you look,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.800
someone’s trying to sell you
something like vitamins,

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.040
yoga mats, blenders, drugs.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.680
Well, breathe a
sigh of relief

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.760
because all we bring
you are the facts.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.640
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.360
Today we explore the power
of the mighty mushroom.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.160
Did you know that it may
be even more important

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.040
to include mushrooms (or tempeh)
in our diet as we age?

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.960
Here’s our first story.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.520
Of more than a hundred compounds

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.480
measured in the bloodstreams
of thousands of individuals,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.240
the one most associated with the lowest
rates of disease and death

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.200
was ergothioneine.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.440
Higher blood levels
were associated with lower risk

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.280
of heart disease, stroke, and death

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.920
from all causes put together
over a period of more than 20 years.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.360
Ergothioneine is an unusual amino acid.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.440
Although it was discovered
more than a century ago,

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.280
it was ignored until recently,
when researchers found

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.200
that humans have a highly specific
transporter protein in our bodies

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.840
specifically designed to pull ergothioneine
out of food and into body tissue.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.000
It’s even upregulated
right before meal times.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.160
This suggests that ergothioneine plays
an important physiological role.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.080
But what? Our first clue
was the tissue distribution.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.200
Ergothioneine concentrates
in parts of your body

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.640
where there are lots of free radicals—
the lens of your eye and your liver,

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.880
for example, as well as sensitive tissues
such as bone marrow and semen.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.440
Researchers found it acts
as a cytoprotectant—a cell protector.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.400
Depriving human cells
of ergothioneine

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.320
leads to accelerated
DNA damage and cell death.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.320
Because we can only get it in food,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.360
and there’s toxicity
associated with its depletion,

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.640
Johns Hopkins University
researchers concluded

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.800
that ergothioneine
“may represent a new vitamin.”

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.840
If it were classified as such,
that would make it the first new vitamin

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.040
since the last new vitamin, B12,
was isolated back in 1948.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.640
However, traditional vitamins
are characterized

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.160
by the manifestation of an overt
dietary deficiency disease

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.800
within a short time frame,
and no specific deficiency disease

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.720
has yet been identified.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.840
But perhaps deﬁciency diseases
are staring us in the face.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.680
Low blood levels of ergothioneine
are correlated with increased risk

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.840
of frailty, cardiovascular disease,
cognitive impairment,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.880
dementia, and Parkinson’s disease.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.480
To describe nutrients that may not
necessarily be essential for life

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.840
but may be essential
for long-term health,

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.600
the famous biochemist Bruce Ames
coined the term “longevity vitamin”

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.400
and identified ergothioneine
as a putative candidate.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.600
What are the best dietary sources?

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.600
It’s not made by plants or animals;

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.400
only fungi like mushrooms
and certain soil bacteria.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.600
Plants can suck some up from the soil,
and animals who eat the plants

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.880
can similarly benefit,
but the highest levels by far

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.800
have been reported in mushrooms.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.760
Excessive tillage of the soil,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.400
which is a common practice
in modern agriculture,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.920
can disrupt the mycelial network,
the fungi filaments

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.320
that pass ergothioneine along
to the roots of crops,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.400
leaving mushrooms and tempeh—
a fungi-fermented soybean cake—

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.640
as the only concentrated
dietary sources.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.960
This is on a dry weight basis, though,
and mushrooms are like 90 percent water;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.880
so, if you change this
to prepared wet weight,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.000
tempeh does even better on more
of a per serving basis.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.920
As mushrooms go, shiitake may have
comparable levels to oyster mushrooms,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.400
about five times more
than white button mushrooms,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.440
but may also be five times as expensive—
unless you grow your own.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.520
Oyster mushrooms can grow
in less than two weeks

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.800
with just-add-water kits.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:52.711
Here’s a pic
from my own personal stash.

00:03:52.711 --> 00:03:54.280
Porcinis may lead the pack, though,

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.080
which could explain why Italians
average more than four times

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.680
the average ergothioneine intake
of Americans.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.560
But even just eating a cup a day
of plain white button mushrooms

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.480
can double ergothioneine
concentrations in the blood.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.840
Yes, ergothioneine is associated
with reduced mortality,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.600
but it was also the blood metabolite
most strongly connected

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.800
to a “health conscious food pattern;”

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.160
so, it could just have just been a proxy
for healthier eating.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.560
And look, correlation
doesn’t mean causation.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.160
Instead of low ergothioneine levels
leading to disease,

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.320
maybe disease somehow leads
to lower ergothioneine levels?

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.640
What evidence do we have that we should
go out of our way to eat mushrooms?

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.760
Well, mushrooms can reduce atherosclerosis
in butterfat-fed mice,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.040
and fruit flies fed
a 1 percent oyster mushroom diet

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.960
did show a slight but significant
survival advantage.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.280
And we suspect it’s the ergothioneine,
since you can give it straight

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.120
and still get a life-extension eﬀect.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.240
But what evidence do we have in people?

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.720
Well, mushroom consumption
is associated with a lower risk of cancer,

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.240
driven mainly by lower
breast cancer rates,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.880
and mushroom consumption
is associated with a lower risk

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.360
of dying prematurely
from all causes put together.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.360
I have covered interventional trials
showing, for example,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.440
that eating just a few mushrooms
a day can improve immune function,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.320
but ergothioneine may be an
underrecognized dietary micronutrient

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.960
for healthy aging in other ways as well.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.280
Dietary ergothioneine is known
to cross the blood-brain barrier,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.960
since it can be found
in human cerebrospinal fluid

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.320
and post-mortem brain samples.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.600
Perhaps this is why a study in Singapore
found that those who consumed

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.480
more than two servings
of mushrooms a week

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.000
had less than half the odds of suffering
from mild cognitive impairment,

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.000
compared to less than once a week.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.320
And a study
of more than 10,000 Japanese elders

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.640
found that three or more times
a week mushroom eaters

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.480
had a significantly lower risk
of developing dementia

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.200
over a period of about six years.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.920
With cross-sectional studies
correlating mushroom consumption

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.480
with better brain function,
researchers decided to put it to the test

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.600
using lion’s mane mushroom,
which is especially popular

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.880
in traditional Chinese medicine.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.640
Randomized double-blind,
placebo-controlled trials on people

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.760
with normal cognitive function,
on people with mild cognitive impairment,

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.240
and on those with early-stage
Alzheimer’s disease

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.640
did find small cognitive or functional
improvements after months

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.520
of a third to a full teaspoon
of powdered lion’s mane mushroom a day

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.720
compared to placebo.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.880
Interestingly, blood ergothioneine levels
appear to decline after about age 60,

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:26.120
and this decline is tied
to both cognitive decline and frailty.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.480
And this does not appear to be due
to declining mushroom intake with age;

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:32.800
so, perhaps the function
of the ergothioneine transporter

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.560
at the blood-brain barrier
declines with age,

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:40.200
potentially making mushroom intake
all the more beneficial as we grow older.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.120
So, if I was to create
a Dr. Greger’s Baker’s Dozen,

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.720
I would probably add mushrooms
to the list.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:49.640
In our next story,

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:55.280
we look at the power of medicinal
mushrooms for cancer survival.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.120
Can mushrooms be medicinal?
Mushroom-based products

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.360
make up a sizable chunk of the
$50 billion supplement market.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.520
This profitable trade provides a powerful
incentive for companies to test

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.960
the credulity of their customers, and
sadly, unsupported assertions

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:15.120
have come to define the
medical mushroom business.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.480
For example, companies that market herbal
medicines exploit references to studies

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.000
on mice to promote their
mushroom capsules

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.800
for treating all kinds of ailments,

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:28.400
but if you haven't noticed,
we're not mice.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.200
I mean, it wouldn't be surprising if
mushrooms had some potent properties.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.040
After all, fungi are where
we got a bunch of drugs,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.360
not the least of which, penicillin.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:39.760
And also a cholesterol-lowering
drug, lovastatin,

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.920
and the powerful immunosuppressant
drug, cyclosporin.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:46.720
Still don't think a little mushroom
can have pharmacological effects?

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.720
Don't forget they can produce some
of our most powerful poisons.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.920
Some kind of look the part, like
the toxic Carolina false morel,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.680
all toadstooly and such, but others
have more of an angelic look,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.140
indeed literally called the
Destroying Angel—that's its name—

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:11.720
and as little as a teaspoon can
cause a painful, lingering death.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:16.560
So anyway, we should have respect
for the pharmacological potential

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.920
of mushrooms, but what can
they do that's good for us?

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.880
Well, consuming shiitake mushrooms
daily improves human immunity.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:29.000
Giving people just one or two
dried shiitake mushrooms a day—

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:33.080
about the weight equivalent of five
to ten fresh ones—for four weeks

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:37.320
resulted in an increase in proliferation
of gamma-delta T lymphocytes

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.640
and doubled the proliferation
of natural killer cells.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.560
Gamma-delta cells act as a first
line of immunological defense,

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.840
and even better,
natural killer cells kill cancer,

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.000
and the shiitake did all this while
lowering markers of systemic inflammation.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.520
Oyster mushroom extracts don't seem
to work as well, but what we care about

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:01.800
is if mushrooms can actually
affect cancer outcomes.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.560
Shiitakes haven't been tried yet,
but reishi mushrooms have,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.840
after being used as a cancer treatment
throughout Asia for centuries.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:16.280
Reishi mushroom for cancer treatment:
what does the science say?

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:19.680
A meta-analysis of five randomized
controlled trials showed that patients

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.720
who had been given reishi mushroom
supplements along with chemo and

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.680
radiation were more likely to
respond positively compared

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:30.200
to just chemo and radiation alone.
Now although adding a reishi mushroom

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.000
extract improved tumor response rates,
the data failed to demonstrate

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.360
a significant effect on tumor shrinkage
when the mushrooms were used alone.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.800
So they aren't recommended
as a single treatment,

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.360
but rather an adjunct treatment
for patients with advanced cancer.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.040
Now response rate just
means the tumor shrinks.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:53.040
What we care about is whether
or not it actually improves

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.320
survival or quality of life. And
we don't have convincing data

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.320
suggesting reishi mushroom
products improve survival.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.140
But those randomized to
reishi were found to have

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:08.840
a relatively better quality of life, so
that's a win as far as I'm concerned.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.520
What about other mushrooms?

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:13.240
Although whole shiitake mushrooms
haven't been tested yet,

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:17.560
there's a compound that's extracted
from shiitakes called lentinan,

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.440
which is said to have
completely inhibited the growth

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:24.680
of a certain kind of sarcoma in mice.
But in actuality it only worked

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:29.200
in one single strain of mice
and failed in nine others.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:34.080
So are we more like the 90% of
mouse strains in which it didn't work?

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:37.880
We need human trials,
and we finally got them.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:42.000
There are data on nearly 10,000
cancer patients who have been treated

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.480
with the shiitake mushroom extract
injected right into their veins.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.400
What did the researchers find?

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:53.520
There is a compound called lentinan,
extracted from shiitake mushrooms.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:59.160
To get about an ounce you have to
distill around 400 pounds of shiitakes.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.640
That's like 2,000 cups of mushrooms.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:06.080
But then you can inject the compound into
cancer patients and see what happens.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.080
The pooled response from a dozen
small clinical trials found that

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.360
the objective response rate
was significantly improved

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:16.600
when lentinan was added to
chemotherapy regimens for lung cancer.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.560
"Objective response rate"
means like tumor shrinkage,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:23.240
but what we really care about
is survival and quality of life.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:27.200
Does it actually make cancer patients
live any longer or any better?

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:31.480
Well, those in the lentinan group
suffered less chemo-related toxicity

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:32.920
to their gut and bone marrow,

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.760
so that alone might be
reason enough to use it.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:38.360
But what about improving survival?

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.160
I was excited to see that lentinan
evidently could

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.320
significantly improve survival rates
for a type of leukemia. And here it is.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.680
Adding lentinan increased average
survival and reduced cachexia—

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:53.480
which is like cancer-associated muscle
wasting—and improved cage-side health.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.920
Wait, what? How...
Damn it.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:00.480
This was improved survival
for brown Norwegian rats.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:07.000
So that so-called clinical benefit
only applies if you're a veterinarian.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:11.320
A compilation of 17 actual human
clinical studies did find improvements

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.240
in one-year survival in
advanced cancer patients,

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.520
but no significant difference in the
likelihood of living out to two years.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.200
Even the compilations of studies
that purport that lentinan offers a

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:27.515
significant advantage in terms of survival
are talking about statistical significance.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:31.600
Lentinan improved survival
by an average of 25 days.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:36.640
Now, 25 days is 25 days, but we should
evaluate claims made by companies

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:42.480
about the miraculous properties of
medicinal mushrooms very critically.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.960
Lentinan has to be
injected intravenously.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:49.560
What about mushroom extract
supplements you can just take yourself?

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.000
Shiitake mushroom extract is available
through the Internet for the treatment

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.360
of prostate cancer for
approximately $300 a month,

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.840
so it's got to be good, right?

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.720
Men who regularly eat mushrooms
do seem to be at lower risk

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.320
for getting prostate cancer,

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.280
and not apparently just because
they eat less meat or more fruits

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:11.320
and vegetables in general. So why not
give a shiitake mushroom extract a try?

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:17.280
Because it doesn't work—ineffective in
the treatment of clinical prostate cancer.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.960
The results demonstrate that
complementary and alternative

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:24.560
medicine claims can actually be
put to the test. What a concept!

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:27.720
Maybe it should be mandatory before
patients spend large sums of money

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:33.200
on unproven treatments, or in
this case, a disproven treatment.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.320
What about God's mushroom, also
known as the Mushroom of Life

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:41.000
or reishi mushrooms?
Conclusions: no significant

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:45.600
anticancer effects were found,
not even a single partial response.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.720
Maybe we're overthinking it?

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:51.720
Plain white button mushroom
extracts can kill off prostate

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.640
cancer cells,
at least in a petri dish,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:58.000
but so could the fancy God's mushroom,
but that didn't end up working in people.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.160
You don't know if plain white
button mushrooms work or not

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.280
until you put it to the test.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.480
What I like about this study is that
the researchers didn't use

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.360
a proprietary extract. They just
used regular whole mushrooms,

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:12.800
dried and powdered—
the equivalent of a half cup

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.640
to a cup and a half of fresh white
button mushrooms a day—

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:19.200
in other words, a totally doable amount.
They gave them to men

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:22.280
with biochemically recurrent
prostate cancer.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:27.080
What that means is the men had already
gotten a prostatectomy or radiation

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:32.240
in an attempt to cut or burn out all the
cancer, but now it's back and growing,

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:38.120
as evidenced by a rise in PSA levels, an
indicator of prostate cancer progression.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:41.360
Of the 26 patients who got
the button mushroom powder,

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:44.200
four appeared to respond,
meaning they got a drop

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:48.160
in PSA levels by more than
50% after starting the 'shrooms.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:52.240
Now in the majority of cases,
the PSA levels continued to rise,

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:53.920
not dipping at all.

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:58.680
But even if there is only a 1 in 18
chance you'll be like these two

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.360
with a prolonged complete
response that continues to date,

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.800
we're not talking about weighing the
risks of some toxic chemotherapy

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:09.920
for the small chance of benefit; just
eating some inexpensive, easy, tasty,

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:13.114
plain white mushrooms every day.

